  "interval_method": "percentile bootstrap of the OLS slope, 5000 resamples of receptions, seed 1925299. Receptions are treated as independent.",
  "interval_excludes_zero": true,
  "verdict": "Across 2 matches and 1408 labelled pass receptions, the nearest outfield defender was square_in_front 1153 (81.9%), side_on 248 (17.6%), back_to 7 (0.5%). A random heading would be about 25% square/in front, 50% side-on, and 25% back-to, so these defenders usually face the receiver. The pre-specified effect is the slope of possession_epv_for_start on orientation score (0 square_in_front, 1 side_on, 2 back_to): estimate 0.0066004 EPV per step. The 95% interval [0.00352453, 0.0102885] excludes zero, so supported is true. Group means are square/in front 0.0318419 (n=1129), side-on 0.03928843 (n=242), back-to 0.03191429 (n=7). Side-on minus square/in front is 0.00744653 with interval [0.00381404, 0.01136275]. Back-to has n=7, and back-to minus square/in front is 7.239e-05 with interval [-0.00838639, 0.00981961], which includes zero. The slope is the square/in front versus side-on association. Other outcomes whose slope interval excludes zero: pass_reception_epv_for, pass_epv_delta_for, xthreat, xshot_player_possession_start. With only 2 matches this comparison is underpowered; receptions are nested in matches, and the interval does not correct for that.",
